1.4.2 ‘(nodefunc FUNCTION)’
---------------------------

FUNCTION is called with no arguments and must return an ‘org-roam-node’.
Capture is positioned at the node's entry point.

   Use this when the destination node is computed at capture time — for
example, today's daily note.

     '("+" "Daily log" plain
       "*** PROGRESS %?\n\n%a\n"
       :target (nodefunc my-daily-node-function)
       :unnarrowed t)


File: org-roam-gt.info,  Node: (nodefunc+headline FUNCTION HEADLINE),  Next: (node+headline TITLE-OR-ID HEADLINE),  Prev: (nodefunc FUNCTION),  Up: New capture target types

1.4.3 ‘(nodefunc+headline FUNCTION HEADLINE)’
---------------------------------------------

Like ‘nodefunc’, but positions inside a named heading within the node.
The heading is created as a child if it does not exist.

     '("+" "Daily log" plain
       "*** PROGRESS %?\n\n%a\n"
       :target (nodefunc+headline my-daily-node-function "Log")
       :unnarrowed t)


File: org-roam-gt.info,  Node: (node+headline TITLE-OR-ID HEADLINE),  Next: (node+olp TITLE-OR-ID "h1" "h2" ),  Prev: (nodefunc+headline FUNCTION HEADLINE),  Up: New capture target types

1.4.4 ‘(node+headline TITLE-OR-ID HEADLINE)’
--------------------------------------------

Looks up a node by its ID or title, then positions inside a named
heading.  The heading is created as a child if absent.

   If TITLE-OR-ID is ‘nil’, the user is prompted to choose a node
interactively.

     ;; Fixed destination node, fixed heading
     '("c" "Cooking recipe" entry
       "** SOMEDAY %?\n\n%(my-web-link)\n\n"
       :target (node+headline "area-cooking-20240921-012344" "Recipes")
       :unnarrowed t)

     ;; User picks the node, fixed heading
     '("T" "Todo" entry
       "** TODO %?\n%T\n\n%i\n"
       :target (node+headline nil "Actions")
       :unnarrowed t)

     ;; Prompt is narrowed by :filter-fn to nodes whose TODO keyword is PROJ or AREA
     '("E" "Email under a project" entry
       "** TODO %?\n"
       :target (node+headline nil "Emails")
       :filter-fn (lambda (n) (member (org-roam-node-todo n) '("PROJ" "AREA")))
       :unnarrowed t)

   The standard org-capture template property ‘:filter-fn’ works with
every prompting target (‘node’ nil, ‘node+headline’ nil, ‘node+olp’ nil,
‘node+olp+datetree’ nil).  It receives an ‘org-roam-node’ and returns
non-nil to keep it in the candidate pool.


File: org-roam-gt.info,  Node: (node+olp TITLE-OR-ID "h1" "h2" ),  Next: (node+olp+datetree TITLE-OR-ID "h1" "h2" ),  Prev: (node+headline TITLE-OR-ID HEADLINE),  Up: New capture target types

1.4.5 ‘(node+olp TITLE-OR-ID "h1" "h2" ...)’
--------------------------------------------

Like ‘node+headline’ but navigates (or creates) an arbitrary outline
path.  Each heading in the path is found or created in sequence.

   If TITLE-OR-ID is ‘nil’, the user is prompted interactively.  Heading
strings may contain ‘${var}’ template variables.

     '("x" "Example" entry
       "** %?\n"
       :target (node+olp "my-node-id" "Section" "Subsection")
       :unnarrowed t)


File: org-roam-gt.info,  Node: (node+olp+datetree TITLE-OR-ID "h1" "h2" ),  Next: (nodefunc+olp+datetree FUNCTION "h1" "h2" ),  Prev: (node+olp TITLE-OR-ID "h1" "h2" ),  Up: New capture target types

1.4.6 ‘(node+olp+datetree TITLE-OR-ID "h1" "h2" ...)’
-----------------------------------------------------

Positions capture at an org datetree entry beneath the node.  The OLP
headings are optional: pass none to build the datetree directly under
the node, or pass one or more to navigate (or create) intermediate
headings first.

   The datetree is built by ‘org-datetree-find-*-create’ per the
‘:tree-type’ template property (default ‘day’; other values: ‘week’,
‘month’, a list grouping like ‘(year month day)’, or a function that
returns such a list).  Setting ‘:time-prompt t’ asks for the date
interactively; otherwise the current date is used (respecting
‘org-overriding-default-time’).

     ;; Daily log entry under Journal / YYYY / YYYY-MM / YYYY-MM-DD
     '("j" "Journal" plain
       "- %U %?"
       :target (node+olp+datetree "my-journal-id" "Log")
       :unnarrowed t)

     ;; Weekly retrospective, ask for date at capture time
     '("r" "Weekly retro" entry
       "** %?\n"
       :target (node+olp+datetree "my-journal-id")
       :tree-type week
       :time-prompt t
       :unnarrowed t)


File: org-roam-gt.info,  Node: (nodefunc+olp+datetree FUNCTION "h1" "h2" ),  Prev: (node+olp+datetree TITLE-OR-ID "h1" "h2" ),  Up: New capture target types

1.4.7 ‘(nodefunc+olp+datetree FUNCTION "h1" "h2" ...)’
------------------------------------------------------

Like ‘node+olp+datetree’, but the destination node is computed by
FUNCTION at capture time instead of looked up by ID or title.  All other
semantics (optional OLP, ‘:tree-type’, ‘:time-prompt’) are identical.

     '("d" "Daily note" plain
       "- %?"
       :target (nodefunc+olp+datetree my-daily-node-function "Log")
       :unnarrowed t)


