1.7 Refiling to a capture target
================================

‘org-roam-refile’ can only move a subtree to the top of a node.  A
capture template can say ‘(node+headline nil "Actions")’ and land inside
it.  ‘org-roam-gt-refile’ closes that gap: it takes a ‘:target’ in the
same syntax as ‘org-roam-capture-templates’ and refiles the subtree at
point there.

     (org-roam-gt-refile :target '(node+headline nil "Actions")
                         :filter-fn #'my-project-p)

   That prompts for a node — restricted to projects by the filter — and
moves the subtree under that node's "Actions" heading, creating the
heading if the node does not have one.  The destination is the same one
the matching capture template would have used.

   Keyword arguments:

Keyword        Meaning
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
‘:target’      Destination, in ‘:target’ syntax.  Defaults to ‘(node nil)’
‘:node’        Destination node, for targets that would otherwise prompt
‘:filter-fn’   Narrows the node prompt, as in ‘org-roam-node-find’
‘:visit’       When non-nil, leave point at the destination

   Only the node-based target types can be refiled to: ‘node’,
‘nodefunc’, ‘node+headline’, ‘nodefunc+headline’, ‘node+olp’,
‘node+olp+datetree’ and ‘nodefunc+olp+datetree’.  The ‘file’-based types
describe where to create a file, which is not a question a refile can
answer; they raise a user error.

   The destination node is chosen in this order: a title or ID written
into the target, then ‘:node’, then a prompt.  So a wrapper can pass the
node it already has and skip the prompt entirely:

     (defun my-refile-to-inbox ()
       "Refile the subtree at point under the Inbox heading of my inbox node."
       (interactive)
       (org-roam-gt-refile :target '(node+headline "my-inbox-id" "Inbox")))

   Creating a heading that was absent is reported on the echo area — for
both refile and capture — since it usually means a typo in a target or a
refile aimed at the wrong node.

   After the move the destination file is saved, because org-roam
updates its database on save; an unsaved destination holds content the
database does not know about.

   Unlike the capture extensions, this function installs no advice and
works whether or not ‘org-roam-gt-mode’ is enabled.


File: org-roam-gt.info,  Node: One path per file,  Next: Directory renames and deletions,  Prev: Refiling to a capture target,  Up: org-roam-gt

1.8 One path per file
=====================

org-roam follows symlinks deliberately — every backend of
‘org-roam--list-files’ passes ‘-L’ — and normalizes the result with
‘expand-file-name’, which does not resolve them.  A symlink that leads
back into ‘org-roam-directory’ therefore makes one file reachable under
two paths.  Both are indexed, both carry the same node ID, and the
second write of that ID is where duplicate-node errors come from.  The
failure is quiet: the file sweep reports no error, and only some files
break outright.

   With ‘org-roam-gt-mode’ enabled, each file gets one path, in both
directions:

   • ‘org-roam-list-files’ is filtered so aliases collapse onto the
     canonical path.  The result is always a subset of what the finder
     reported — a path that was not listed is never introduced.
   • ‘org-roam-db-update-file’ and ‘org-roam-db-clear-file’ canonicalize
     their file argument, so visiting a file through an alias path and
     saving it records the canonical path rather than reintroducing the
     duplicate the sweep just removed.

   The canonical path is the physical location when it lies inside
‘org-roam-directory’.  When it lies outside — a link such as
‘links/notes’ pointing at a tree kept elsewhere — the in-tree path is
kept unchanged, since rewriting it would move the file out of org-roam's
scope.  Where neither applies, the choice is deterministic (shortest
path, then alphabetical), because an unstable choice would make org-roam
treat the file as moved on alternating syncs.

   ‘M-x org-roam-gt-report-aliased-files’ lists what is being collapsed,
reading the file list with canonicalization disabled so the topology
stays visible:

     3511 files listed, 3326 distinct, 185 reachable by several paths

     /roam/papers/lee2017understanding.org
         also as: /roam/proj/2026-oss-and-ai/dmg/papers/lee2017understanding.org

   Set ‘org-roam-gt-enable-path-canonicalization’ to nil to leave
org-roam's behaviour untouched.


File: org-roam-gt.info,  Node: Directory renames and deletions,  Next: Citations without a whole-buffer parse,  Prev: One path per file,  Up: org-roam-gt

1.9 Directory renames and deletions
===================================

‘org-roam-db-autosync-mode’ keeps the database current by advising
‘rename-file’ and ‘delete-file’, both of which act on one file.  A whole
directory reaches neither: the rename handler tests its arguments with
‘org-roam-file-p’, which a directory fails, and a directory is removed
by ‘delete-directory’, which carries no advice at all.  Move or delete a
directory of notes and every file it held keeps its rows, naming a path
that no longer exists, until the next ‘org-roam-db-sync’.

   With ‘org-roam-gt-mode’ enabled:

   • Renaming a directory inside ‘org-roam-directory’ clears the rows
     recorded under the old path and indexes the Org files found under
     the new one.  A directory moved out of the tree is only cleared:
     nothing outside ‘org-roam-directory’ is indexed.
   • Deleting a directory clears the rows of the files that are actually
     gone once the deletion returns, so a deletion that fails part way
     does not cost live rows.  Trashed files count as deleted — they are
     no longer in the tree.

   Both operations are wrapped rather than followed, because the rows
recorded under a directory can only be read while it still exists.
Renaming a single file is left to org-roam's own advice, and a directory
outside ‘org-roam-directory’ never reaches the database at all.

   Set ‘org-roam-gt-track-directory-operations’ to nil to leave
org-roam's behaviour untouched.


File: org-roam-gt.info,  Node: Citations without a whole-buffer parse,  Next: Speed commands,  Prev: Directory renames and deletions,  Up: org-roam-gt

1.10 Citations without a whole-buffer parse
===========================================

Saving a large file is slow, and most of the time goes to work whose
result is thrown away.  ‘org-roam-db-update-file’ calls
‘org-element-parse-buffer’ on every file it indexes and passes the tree
to a single reader, ‘org-roam-db-map-citations’.  Files that contain no
citation — the large majority in most collections — pay for the tree and
get nothing from it.

   ‘org-roam-db-map-links’ shows the cheaper approach for the same kind
of work: search for the syntax with a regexp, then call
‘org-element-context’ at each hit.  With ‘org-roam-gt-mode’ enabled
citations are found that way, and the parse is skipped, since nothing
reads its result any more.  A tree is still built for the span of each
citation actually found, which keeps the objects and their buffer
positions identical to the ones a whole-buffer parse produces.

   Text that only looks like a citation, such as ‘[cite:@key]’ inside a
source block, is context that answers something other than a citation
and is passed over — the same outcome as parsing the whole buffer.

   Measured on a 312 KB file with 53 nodes and no citations, re-indexing
went from 1.34 s to 0.86 s.  Re-indexing the 127 files that do contain
citations produced rows identical to those the parse-based walk had
stored.

   Set ‘org-roam-gt-scan-citations’ to nil to restore org-roam's
behaviour.  The scanning walk and the skipped parse are one setting on
purpose: skipping the parse while org-roam's own walk is in place would
report no citations at all.


File: org-roam-gt.info,  Node: Speed commands,  Next: Faster node search,  Prev: Citations without a whole-buffer parse,  Up: org-roam-gt

1.11 Speed commands
===================

When ‘org-roam-gt-transient.el’ is loaded (see the opt-in section
above), the key ‘m’ is added to ‘org-speed-commands’.  It opens a
‘transient’ prefix with shortcuts for common org-roam operations:

Key   Action
-----------------------------------------
‘c’   Capture (‘org-roam-capture’)
‘f’   Find node (‘org-roam-node-find’)
‘r’   Refile to a node
‘x’   Extract subtree

   Speed commands are available when point is at the beginning of a
heading.


File: org-roam-gt.info,  Node: Faster node search,  Next: Node list buffer,  Prev: Speed commands,  Up: org-roam-gt

1.12 Faster node search
=======================

When you invoke ‘org-roam-node-find’, Emacs has to build the list of
every node in your database before you can start typing.  On a large
database this pause is long enough to interrupt your flow.  The delay
comes from how each node's display line is constructed: org-roam uses a
format string with ‘%’-placeholders, and assembling that string for
every node on every invocation is slow.

   ‘org-roam-gt’ replaces the format string with a plain Lisp function
that produces the same display but runs faster, which shortens the pause
noticeably.  The completion interface looks and behaves identically.
The original template is restored if you disable the mode.


File: org-roam-gt.info,  Node: Node list buffer,  Next: Development,  Prev: Faster node search,  Up: org-roam-gt

1.13 Node list buffer
=====================

Org-roam shows you one node at a time.  ‘org-roam-node-find’ needs you
to know what you are looking for before it shows you anything, and there
is no view of the database as a whole: no way to see what is in it, to
put the notes you touched this week at the top, or to answer a question
like "what do I have tagged ‘jp’ that is still a TODO?".

   ‘M-x org-roam-gt-list’ opens ‘*Org Roam Nodes*’, a listing of every
node in the database, one node per row.  From it you can:

   • *browse* the database — all of it, sorted however you like;
   • *search* it by tag, TODO keyword, outline level, title, or file
     name, combining those conditions until the rows left are the ones
     you want;
   • *open* any node you find, in this window or another;
   • *save* a search you use often and return to it later.

   No command in the buffer changes a node, a file, or the database.
You can leave it open while you edit your notes, and press ‘g’ when you
want it to catch up.

   Nothing needs to be loaded or enabled first: the command is part of
‘org-roam-gt’ and is available as soon as the package is, whether or not
‘org-roam-gt-mode’ is on.

* Menu:

* What each row shows::
* Finding what you want::
* Keys::
* Saving a view you use often::
* Choosing the columns::
* Adding your own search condition::


File: org-roam-gt.info,  Node: What each row shows,  Next: Finding what you want,  Up: Node list buffer

1.13.1 What each row shows
--------------------------

Five columns are shown by default.  Five more are available and hidden:

Column        Shows                                               Width   Shown
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
‘todo’        TODO keyword                                        6       yes
‘date’        when the node's file was last modified              10      yes
‘tags’        the node's tags, separated by commas                20      yes
‘priority’    priority letter, from a ‘[#A]’ cookie               1       no
‘scheduled’   scheduled date                                      10      no
‘deadline’    deadline date                                       10      no
‘level’       outline level; 0 for a whole-file node              1       no
‘olp’         the headings above this one                         30      no
‘title’       the node's title                                    50      yes
‘file’        the node's file, relative to ‘org-roam-directory’   30      yes

   Two things about ‘date’ are worth knowing before you sort by it.  It
is the modification time of the *file*, not of the node, so every node
in one file shows the same date; and it is what the list is sorted by
when you open it, newest first, which is usually the fastest way back to
whatever you were working on yesterday.

   A value too long for its column is cut short and ends with ‘…’, so
you can always tell that you are seeing part of it.  The exception is
the right-most column, which has the rest of the window available and is
shown in full — which is why ‘file’ is placed last by default, where
long paths stay readable.


File: org-roam-gt.info,  Node: Finding what you want,  Next: Keys,  Prev: What each row shows,  Up: Node list buffer

1.13.2 Finding what you want
----------------------------

  1. Sorting

     Click a column heading to sort by it, or press ‘s’ to move the sort
     to the next column.  Clicking the heading you are already sorted by
     reverses the order.

  2. Searching

     Press ‘/’ and choose what to search on:

     Search on           Matches nodes whose…
     ---------------------------------------------------------------
     ‘by-tag’            tags include one you name
     ‘by-todo’           TODO keyword is one you name
     ‘by-level’          level is whole-file, heading, or either
     ‘by-scheduled’      scheduled date falls in a range you give
     ‘by-deadline’       deadline falls in a range you give
     ‘by-title-regexp’   title matches a regular expression
     ‘by-file-regexp’    file name matches a regular expression
     ‘unfilter’          (removes every search condition)

     The tag and TODO prompts offer only the values your nodes actually
     use, so you cannot pick one that matches nothing.

     Two ways of combining conditions are available, and they do
     opposite things:

        • *Naming several values in one prompt matches more nodes.*  The
          tag, TODO, and level prompts accept a comma-separated list,
          and a node matches if *any* value does.  Answering ‘jp,ww’ at
          the tag prompt gives you everything tagged either way.

        • *Pressing ‘/’ again matches fewer nodes.*  Each condition
          applies on top of those already in effect.  Searching by tag
          and then by level leaves the nodes that satisfy both.  You can
          use the same condition twice: two separate tag searches for
          ‘jp’ and ‘ww’ leave the nodes tagged *both*, where one tag
          search for ‘jp,ww’ leaves those tagged *either*.

     With a prefix argument, ‘C-u /’ excludes instead of includes: ‘C-u
     /’ ‘by-todo’ ‘DONE’ hides finished nodes.  Excluding a multi-value
     condition excludes all of them together, so excluding tags ‘jp,ww’
     leaves the nodes tagged neither.

     Some examples:

     To see…                                 Do
     -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
     everything tagged ‘jp’                  ‘/’ ‘by-tag’ ‘jp’
     everything tagged ‘jp’ or ‘ww’          ‘/’ ‘by-tag’ ‘jp,ww’
     notes tagged both ‘jp’ and ‘ww’         ‘/’ ‘by-tag’ ‘jp’, then ‘/’ ‘by-tag’ ‘ww’
     unfinished work, done items hidden      ‘C-u /’ ‘by-todo’ ‘DONE’
     headings tagged ‘jp’, not whole files   ‘/’ ‘by-tag’ ‘jp’, then ‘/’ ‘by-level’ ‘heading’
     notes whose file is under ‘daily/’      ‘/’ ‘by-file-regexp’ ‘daily/’
     deadlines already past                  ‘/’ ‘by-deadline’ ‘overdue’
     everything outstanding now              ‘/’ ‘by-deadline’ ‘due’
     what is scheduled in the coming week    ‘/’ ‘by-scheduled’ ‘next-7d’
     notes with a deadline but no schedule   ‘/’ ‘by-deadline’ ‘any’, then ‘C-u /’ ‘by-scheduled’ ‘any’

     The regular-expression searches take a single pattern rather than a
     list, because a comma is an ordinary character in a regular
     expression.  Press ‘/’ again to add a second pattern.

  3. Searching by scheduled date or deadline

     ‘by-scheduled’ and ‘by-deadline’ ask for a date range.  Six names
     are offered for the cases that come up most often:

     Name           Matches a date that is
     ----------------------------------------------------------
     ‘overdue’      earlier than today
     ‘due’          today or earlier
     ‘today’        today
     ‘next-7d’      today or within the following seven days
     ‘this-week’    in the current week, Monday to Sunday
     ‘this-month’   in the current calendar month
     ‘any’          set to anything at all

     For anything else, type a range as ‘FROM,TO’:

     Range           Matches a date that is
     -------------------------------------------------------------
     ‘-3d,+5d’       between three days ago and five days ahead
     ‘,today’        today or earlier — the same as ‘due’
     ‘+1d,+7d’       in the coming week, not counting today
     ‘2026-01-01,’   on or after 1 January 2026

     Either side may be left empty, which means no limit in that
     direction.  Both ends are included in the range.  Each side is
     written in org's own date syntax, so a relative offset (‘+3d’,
     ‘-2w’, ‘+1m’), an absolute date (‘2026-01-01’), a weekday name
     (‘mon’), or ‘.’ for today all work.  A date that cannot be read is
     reported when you enter it.

     A node with no scheduled date or no deadline never matches a range,
     so ‘any’ selects exactly those that have one — and ‘C-u /’
     ‘by-deadline’ ‘any’ selects those that do not.

     The range is stored as you typed it and worked out afresh each time
     it is applied, not once when you enter it.  A bookmark of
     "deadlines in the next seven days" therefore still shows the next
     seven days when you return to it next month.  Combine the two to
     answer a question about both dates at once:

          /  by-scheduled  next-7d
          /  by-deadline   overdue

     The mode line shows the conditions in effect and how many of your
     nodes are left:

          -:--- *Org Roam Nodes*   [tag=jp,ww !todo=DONE] 25/2850

     Press ‘D’ to drop one condition, choosing from those in effect, or
     ‘V’ to clear them all and return the buffer to its defaults.


