1.13.3 Keys
-----------

Key         Does
---------------------------------------------------------
‘RET’       open the node in this window
‘o’         open the node in another window
‘TAB’       show the node in another window, stay here
‘i’         show the node's raw database record
‘s’         sort by the next column
‘/’         add a search condition
‘C-u /’     add a condition that excludes
‘D’         drop one search condition
‘c’         show or hide a column
‘C’         choose all the columns, and their order
‘V’         clear the search and restore the columns
‘g’         re-read the database
‘C-x r m’   save this view as a bookmark
‘q’         bury the buffer

   Sorting, searching, and changing columns all work from a copy of the
node list taken when the buffer was opened, so they are immediate no
matter how large your database is.  Press ‘g’ to pick up notes you have
added or edited since.


File: org-roam-gt.info,  Node: Saving a view you use often,  Next: Choosing the columns,  Prev: Keys,  Up: Node list buffer

1.13.4 Saving a view you use often
----------------------------------

If you keep coming back to the same search — say your unfinished
Japanese notes — save it with ‘C-x r m’ (‘bookmark-set’) and give it a
name.  The name offered already describes the search:

     Org roam nodes: tag=jp !todo=DONE

   Jumping to that bookmark later opens the list, restores the columns,
the search conditions, and the sort order, and re-reads the database, so
you see the nodes that match *now* rather than the ones that matched
when you saved it.  These are ordinary Emacs bookmarks; anything that
works with ‘bookmark.el’ can store and jump to them.

   To keep the columns, search, and sort order of the buffer between
Emacs sessions as well:

     (setq org-roam-gt-list-persist-state t)


File: org-roam-gt.info,  Node: Choosing the columns,  Next: Adding your own search condition,  Prev: Saving a view you use often,  Up: Node list buffer

1.13.5 Choosing the columns
---------------------------

To change which columns you see, and in what order:

     (setq org-roam-gt-list-columns '(todo date tags title file))

   Inside the buffer, ‘c’ shows or hides a single column and ‘C’ sets
the whole list at once.  A column you turn on appears to the left of
‘title’ and ‘file’, which stay on the right where you expect to read
them.

   To give a column more room:

     (setq org-roam-gt-list-column-widths '((title . 80) (file . 60)))

   The default widths fit the first four columns into roughly 90
characters and leave the rest of the window to the last one.  To change
how dates are written, set ‘org-roam-gt-list-date-format’; keep the year
first if you want sorting by date to stay chronological.


File: org-roam-gt.info,  Node: Adding your own search condition,  Prev: Choosing the columns,  Up: Node list buffer

1.13.6 Adding your own search condition
---------------------------------------

The searches offered by ‘/’ are entries in
‘org-roam-gt-list-filter-alist’, and you can add your own.  An entry
names a function to ask the user for values and a function to test one
node against one value:

     (add-to-list 'org-roam-gt-list-filter-alist
                  (cons 'by-alias
                        (list :name "Alias"
                              :reader (lambda ()
                                        (list (read-string "Alias: ")))
                              :predicate (lambda (node alias)
                                           (member alias (org-roam-node-aliases node)))
                              :doc "Nodes that have the given alias.")))

   The reader returns a list, even for a single value; the predicate is
asked about one value at a time.  Matching any of several values,
excluding with ‘C-u’, and appearing in the mode line are handled for
you.


File: org-roam-gt.info,  Node: Development,  Next: MELPA submission,  Prev: Node list buffer,  Up: org-roam-gt

1.14 Development
================

The repository ships a ‘Makefile’ that drives every dev workflow via a
project-local ‘.elpa/’ (nothing touches your user package directory):

     make              # byte-compile
     make test         # run the buttercup test suite
     make lint         # package-lint
     make checkdoc     # checkdoc (errors on any warning)
     make check-declare  # verify declare-function arguments
     make check        # compile + lint + checkdoc + check-declare
     make clean        # remove *.elc

   The GitHub Actions workflow at ‘.github/workflows/package-lint.yml’
runs ‘make lint’, ‘make checkdoc’, ‘make check-declare’, and ‘make test’
on Emacs 30.1, 31.1, and snapshot.


File: org-roam-gt.info,  Node: MELPA submission,  Prev: Development,  Up: org-roam-gt

1.15 MELPA submission
=====================

The MELPA recipe lives at ‘melpa/org-roam-gt’; instructions for
submitting it upstream are in ‘melpa/README.md’.


